<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">15 December in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 15 December, from 37 to 2001.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">37 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,989 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The future emperor Nero is born at Antium</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lucius Domitius Ahenobarbus, later the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>, was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antium">Antium</a> to Agrippina the Younger. Adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, he became emperor at 16 and was the last of the Julio-Claudian dynasty.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/533">533 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,493 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Belisarius defeats the Vandal king Gelimer at Tricamarum</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">West of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, the Byzantine army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> defeated the Vandal king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a>, whose brother Tzazon was killed. Gelimer fled to the mountains and surrendered in 534, ending the Vandal Kingdom and restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tricamarum">Battle of Tricamarum</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelimer">Gelimer</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1025">1025 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,001 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Constantine VIII becomes sole emperor after 63 years as co-emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Co-emperor in name since 962, alongside his father Romanos II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>, Constantine VIII became sole emperor on Basil's death. He ruled alone until 1028.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1161">1161 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">865 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Jin emperor Wanyan Liang is assassinated by his own officers</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After the Jin defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caishi">Battle of Caishi</a>, officers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a> rebelled and killed him in his camp. His successor posthumously demoted him to the rank of prince.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars">Jin–Song wars</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caishi">Battle of Caishi</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1791">1791 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">235 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Bill of Rights is ratified</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>'s approval gave the first ten amendments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">US Constitution</a> the required three-quarters of states. They guarantee freedoms of speech, religion, press and assembly, and rights to due process.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1890">1890 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">136 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sitting Bull is killed at Standing Rock</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Lakota leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a>, a leader at the victory over Custer at the Little Bighorn, was shot dead by Indian police trying to arrest him. Two weeks later came the massacre at Wounded Knee.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1939">1939 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">87 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Gone with the Wind premieres in Atlanta</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The film of Margaret Mitchell's novel premiered at Loew's Grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in Atlanta. It won eight competitive Academy Awards, and allowing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> it remains the highest-grossing film ever.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/pisa" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pisa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2001">2001 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">25 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Leaning Tower of Pisa reopens</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After eleven years of closure and stabilisation work, the Leaning Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> reopened to visitors. Engineers had reduced its tilt by about 44 centimetres, securing it for centuries.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 15 December <span class="n">3</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/albert-iv-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Albert IV, Duke of Bavaria</span><span class="tp">1447–1508</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pehr-evind-svinhufvud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pehr-evind-svinhufvud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pehr Evind Svinhufvud</span><span class="tp">1861–1944</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emilio-jacinto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emilio-jacinto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Emilio Jacinto</span><span class="tp">1875–1899</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
